index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..ac684898386c608df36563dcfa62c8402ac62615
--- /dev/null
+++ b/contrib/chronik-parity/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,294 @@
+# Chronik parity harness
+
+Tools that compare two Chronik builds on the same deterministic regtest chain
+(design C1, section 8.3). Implementation A is normally the Rust Chronik and
+B the native C++ Chronik, but any two builds of the same chain can be used.
+For example, A and B can be the same binary when validating the harness.
+
+| File | Purpose |
+|------|---------|
+| `parity_chain.py` | Runs the chain on two nodes, drives the corpus at checkpoints and writes the report |
+| `corpus.py` | Builds the HTTP and WebSocket request corpus from a run record; it can also run standalone against two running Chronik instances |
+| `compare.py` | Compares responses, WebSocket sessions and DB dumps; it can also compare two saved response files |
+
+## Requirements
+
+- Two CMake build dirs. Each needs `src/bitcoind` or `src/lotusd` and
+  `test/config.ini`, and both must be built with Chronik.
+- Python with `protobuf` and `websocket-client`, plus `chronik_pb2`. The
+  harness finds `chronik_pb2` on `PYTHONPATH`, via `--pb2-dir`, or by running
+  `protoc` on `<srcdir>/chronik/chronik-proto/proto/chronik.proto`.
+
+For example:
+
+```
+python3 -m venv venv && venv/bin/pip install protobuf websocket-client
+PY=venv/bin/python
+```
+
+## Running
+
+```
+# eCash tier: Rust (A) vs native (B), 400 blocks, the design's full run
+$PY contrib/chronik-parity/parity_chain.py \
+    --build-a build/ecash-rust --build-b build/ecash-native --blocks 400
+
+# Quick check
+$PY contrib/chronik-parity/parity_chain.py \
+    --build-a build/ecash-rust --build-b build/ecash-rust --blocks 120
+
+# V2 tier (v7 transactions, no SLP/ALP; the V2 tier needs --no-tokens)
+$PY contrib/chronik-parity/parity_chain.py \
+    --build-a build/v2-rust --build-b build/v2-native --no-tokens --blocks 120
+
+# With DB dump comparison (chronik-native-dump: table\tkey_hex\tvalue_hex, sorted)
+$PY contrib/chronik-parity/parity_chain.py ... \
+    --dump-tool build/native/chronik/native/chronik-native-dump \
+    [--dump-mode stopped|live] [--dump-live-arg --readonly]
+```
+
+### Options
+
+| Option | Effect |
+|--------|--------|
+| `--blocks N` | Chain length (≥ 110; default 400) |
+| `--checkpoint-interval N` | Blocks between checkpoints (default 50) |
+| `--corpus-mode light` | At intermediate checkpoints, query only blocks/txs from the last 60 blocks (the final checkpoint always uses the full corpus) |
+| `--max-scripts N` | Cap on the scripts queried per checkpoint (default 400). The V2 tier encodes the value into each output, so almost every output is a separate script |
+| `--no-tokens` | No SLP/ALP scenarios, and passes `-chroniktokenindex=0` |
+| `--tier auto/ecash/v2` | Transaction format (auto: `v2` when the binary is `lotusd`) |
+| `--miner separate/a` | Which node builds the blocks (see "Block production" below) |
+| `--no-ws`, `--no-http`, `--no-broadcast` | Skip parts of the run |
+| `--allow PATH` | Add an accepted difference (fnmatch on the field path) |
+| `--extra-arg-a/-b ARG` | Extra node arguments, e.g. for negative controls |
+| `--save-responses` | Keep every raw response pair under `<tmpdir>/responses/` |
+| `--keep-going` | Do not stop at the first checkpoint with differences |
+| `--seed N` | Scenario RNG seed (default 7). `--randomseed` (framework) defaults to 1 |
+| `--portseed N` | Framework port seed. Ports are always ≥ 20000, so they never touch the live ports 10604/10605/8331/18331/19331/11604/12604 |
+| `--tmpdir DIR` | Run dir (default `$CHRONIK_PARITY_TMPDIR/run-*`, or `<system temp>/chronik-parity-runs/run-*`). It is kept after the run unless `--cleanup` is given |
+
+The run dir contains:
+
+- `report.json`: the result;
+- `records/<checkpoint>.json`: the objects seen;
+- `dumps/`: DB dumps, when a dump tool is given;
+- `responses/`: raw responses, with `--save-responses`;
+- the node datadirs.
+
+The exit status is 0 only if there are no unexpected differences. All nodes
+are stopped (or killed on failure) at exit.
+
+### Standalone corpus and compare
+
+```
+# Run a recorded corpus against two running Chronik instances
+$PY contrib/chronik-parity/corpus.py --record RUN/records/h401-final.json \
+    --url-a http://127.0.0.1:PA --url-b http://127.0.0.1:PB \
+    [--ws-static] [--save-responses DIR] [--report r.json]
+
+# Compare two saved response files
+$PY contrib/chronik-parity/compare.py DIR/cli.A.jsonl DIR/cli.B.jsonl --report r.json
+```
+
+## What a run covers
+
+### Chain stream
+
+- **Block production.** By default (`--miner separate`), a third node without
+  Chronik, built from `--build-a`, builds every block. A and B both receive
+  each block through `submitblock`, and every tx through
+  `sendrawtransaction`, in the same order and with the same `setmocktime`.
+  `--miner a` mines on A and submits to B, as the design text describes.
+  On the V2 Rust tier this is not symmetric (see "Known issues" below).
+- **Isolation.** The nodes are never connected.
+- **Settings.** All nodes run with `-acceptnonstdtxn=1` and
+  `-chronikscripthashindex=1`. `-avalanche=0` is added where the option
+  exists, so the run is avalanche-free.
+- **Scripts.** Coinbases rotate over P2SH(OP_TRUE), P2PKH, P2PK (33 and 65
+  bytes), bare OP_TRUE, an "other" script, and P2SH with a scriptSig LOKAD
+  push. Fan-outs create every script kind, and signed P2PKH/P2PK outputs are
+  spent again.
+- **Mempool.** The run builds mempool chains and consolidations. Unconfirmed
+  txs remain at the checkpoints.
+- **OP_RETURN.** Outputs include LOKAD pushes (`PRTY`, `TEST`), eMPP
+  sections (`LKD1`, `LKD2`), bare OP_RETURN, a short push and a non-LOKAD
+  push.
+- **Tokens.** These scenarios run in three passes on full runs:
+  - SLP fungible: genesis, mint, send that burns 10, invalid overspend, BURN,
+    a parse failure, and an unknown token type.
+  - NFT1: group genesis, group fan-out, two child geneses, child send, and a
+    child genesis without a group input.
+  - SLP mint vault: vault setup, genesis, mint before the vault confirms
+    ("Missing MINT vault"), and a valid vault mint.
+  - ALP: genesis with data and auth pubkey, mint, genesis+send in one tx,
+    intentional burn+send, overspend, invalid/unknown sections, an SLP tx
+    spending ALP tokens, and a mint with 2 batons.
+- **Conflicts.** A mempool tx and its child are double-spent by a block
+  (`generateblock`) every few rounds.
+- **Reorgs.** Each run includes four:
+  - R1: `invalidateblock` on all nodes, then a longer fork and
+    `reconsiderblock`.
+  - R2: a competing chain built in Python with a double spend, submitted to
+    both nodes. On the V2 tier this falls back to R1.
+  - R3: `invalidateblock` of depth 3, a mempool tx, then `reconsiderblock`.
+  - R4: replace the tip, then reconsider the stale tip.
+- **Final broadcast phase.** These requests change the mempool. Each is sent
+  identically to A and then to B:
+  - a valid tx, twice (the second time it is already known);
+  - a token-burning tx that fails token checks, then the same tx with
+    `skip_token_checks`;
+  - a batch with a parent and its child;
+  - a broadcast with `finalization_timeout_secs=1`;
+  - a conflicting batch.
+
+### HTTP corpus at each checkpoint
+
+The corpus runs every 50 blocks, at the end, and also at height 1, where it
+opens the first WebSocket stretch. Every route of `chronik-http/src/server.rs`
+is queried:
+
+- **Info:** `/blockchain-info` (GET and HEAD), `/chronik-info`,
+  `/unconfirmed-txs`.
+- **Blocks:**
+  - `/block`, `/block-header` (also with `checkpoint_height`) and `/block-txs`
+    for every height and hash;
+  - stale (reorged-out) block hashes;
+  - invalid heights and hashes;
+  - `/blocks` and `/block-headers` ranges, including the 500-block limit,
+    reversed and negative ranges, and `checkpoint_height` variants.
+- **Txs:** `/tx`, `/raw-tx` and `/token` for every txid seen. This includes
+  conflicted txs and txs from stale blocks, plus malformed txids.
+- **Groups:** `/script/:type/:payload/{history,confirmed-txs,unconfirmed-txs,utxos}`
+  for every script, with the scripthash variant. `/token-id/...` covers all
+  four sub-routes and `/lokad-id/...` all three.
+- **Pagination edges** for history routes:
+  - page 0;
+  - page_size 1, 200, 201 and 0;
+  - malformed and huge values;
+  - the last page and one page past it, computed from A's first response;
+  - `page=num_txs-1&page_size=1`.
+- **Malformed groups:** wrong lengths, unknown types, an oversized "other"
+  script.
+- **Plugin routes** (disabled), and `/pause` and `/resume` (403).
+- **Fallback and methods:** unknown paths, wrong methods (405), OPTIONS on
+  every POST route, and GET `/ws` without an upgrade.
+- **POST bodies:**
+  - good, bad, missing, charset-suffixed, upper-case and non-UTF-8
+    Content-Types;
+  - empty, garbage and truncated protobuf;
+  - a 2,000,000-byte body and a 2,000,001-byte body (413).
+- **`/validate-tx`:** never-sent valid txs, a token-burning tx, an overspend,
+  sampled mempool and confirmed txs, and junk.
+- **`/broadcast-tx(s)`** (no mempool change): confirmed txs, mempool txs,
+  junk, an empty batch, and finalization timeouts over the limit.
+- **`/script/batch/{utxos,summary}`:** every script up to the limit, exactly
+  500, 501 (limit), empty, duplicates, bad type, bad length, scripthash.
+
+The full 400-block run sends about 85,000 requests to each node.
+
+### WebSocket corpus
+
+At every checkpoint the scripted sessions are opened on both nodes, with
+identical subscriptions. They record every data frame until the next
+checkpoint, where they are quiesced and compared:
+
+| Session | Mode |
+|---------|------|
+| `blocks`, `txs` | strict |
+| one session per script, token id, LOKAD id, scripthash and txid (up to 60) | strict |
+| an "errors" session: text frame, garbage, plugin sub, bad type, bad length, oversized script (> 201 bytes), bad token id or txid, short LOKAD id, unsub without sub, empty frame, then a blocks sub | strict |
+| `scripts-all`, `tokens-all`, `lokad-all` | set |
+| `mixed` (duplicate subs, sub/unsub, every type) | set |
+
+"strict" means the message sequence must be identical. "set" means the set of
+distinct messages must be identical.
+
+### DB dumps (optional)
+
+When `--dump-tool` is given, the tool runs at every checkpoint as
+`TOOL [--readonly] <datadir>/regtest/indexes/chronik`. Its stdout must be
+sorted `table\tkey_hex\tvalue_hex` lines, and the A and B dumps are compared
+line by line.
+
+- `--dump-mode stopped` (the default) stops all nodes for a consistent dump,
+  then restarts them with the same mocktime.
+- `--dump-mode live` dumps while the nodes run. The tool must open the env
+  read-only (MDBX read transaction).
+
+## Comparison rules and accepted differences
+
+- **Classes.** Every HTTP pair falls into one of:
+  - `identical`: same status, content type, other headers (except `Date`)
+    and raw bytes;
+  - `parsed_equal`: the bytes differ, but the parsed protobuf messages differ
+    only in allow-listed field paths;
+  - `different`.
+- **Message type.** The expected message is known per route; non-200
+  protobuf bodies are parsed as `Error`.
+- **Field paths.** Paths look like `TxHistoryPage.txs[3].inputs[0].sats`.
+  Allow patterns match the indexed path or the path without indices.
+- **Accepted differences:**
+  - `ChronikInfo.version` (`FormatFullVersion()` of the host build).
+  - The §1 defects (V2 spent-coin txid, per-IP key) are not exercised here.
+    Add them with `--allow` once their field paths are known.
+- **Two kinds of WebSocket nondeterminism are inherent to Chronik:**
+  1. A session merges its subscriptions with `tokio::select!` and
+     `select_all`, and drops consecutive identical messages. The interleaving
+     across different subscriptions of one session is therefore
+     scheduling-dependent, even for one binary. The harness compares
+     multi-subscription sessions as sets, and gives every subscription its
+     own strict session.
+  2. Each subscription channel has a fixed capacity (blocks 16, txs 32,
+     groups 16), and overflow silently drops messages, depending on timing.
+     The feeder keeps every burst well below capacity:
+     - blocks hold at most 6 txs (`generateblock` with ancestor-closed
+       chunks);
+     - change scripts rotate;
+     - reorgs are at most 3 blocks deep;
+     - WS sessions are quiesced after every block or reorg event.
+
+## Validation runs
+
+All runs below use `build/ecash-rust` or `build/v2-rust` for both A and B
+(same binary), with a stub dump tool where noted.
+
+| Run | Blocks | HTTP requests | WS sessions | Dumps | Result |
+|-----|--------|---------------|-------------|-------|--------|
+| eCash, miner on A | 400 | 74,312 identical | 474 identical (16,783 msgs) | stub dump, 10 equal | 0 differences |
+| eCash, separate miner, 3 token passes | 400 | 85,766 identical | 469 identical (15,597 msgs) | stub dump, 10 equal | 0 differences (850 txs, 25 conflicts, 4 reorgs, 51 token steps) |
+| eCash, miner on A | 120 | 10,090 identical | 72 identical | stub dump | 0 differences |
+| eCash, separate miner | 120 | 10,096 identical | 72 identical | – | 0 differences |
+| V2, `--no-tokens`, separate miner | 120 | 17,322 identical | 66 identical | – | 0 differences |
+| V2, `--no-tokens`, separate miner | 400 | ~40k identical per checkpoint through h402 | 66 identical per checkpoint | – | 0 differences through h402; final phase hung on node A (see Known issues) |
+| V2, `--miner a` | 110 | 270 differences | 5 differences | – | coinbase script asymmetry (see Known issues) |
+| Negative control: B with `-chroniklokadidindex=0` | 110 | 196 `/lokad-id` differences and 1 WS difference | | | detected, exit 1 |
+
+## Known issues
+
+- **V2 Rust tier, `--miner a`.** The node that mines a block indexes its
+  coinbase output under a different script than a node that receives the same
+  block via `submitblock`. For example,
+  `/script/other/0000f2052a01…/history` returns 0 txs on the miner and 25 on
+  the receiver; WS script sessions differ the same way. The mined block's
+  in-memory outputs keep the legacy template script, while deserialized
+  blocks carry the V2 output encoding. This is why `--miner separate` is the
+  default. It is probably a Rust V2 defect worth a separate look, since the
+  index depends on how the block arrived.
+- **V2 400-block run: node A stopped responding during the broadcast phase.**
+  Every checkpoint through height 402 was identical. Then the first
+  `POST /broadcast-tx` to A timed out after 60 s. A never logged mempool
+  PreChecks for the tx, and after 13:36:18 it logged no further RPC
+  activity. The next `submitblock` to A then hit the 240 s RPC timeout. B,
+  running the same binary, answered every broadcast (504 and 200/400
+  responses). The process was alive, so this looks like an intermittent
+  lock-up in the Rust broadcast path on lotusd, such as a lock-order
+  inversion between the indexer lock and cs_main or the validation queue. It
+  did not happen in the 120-block V2 run. No stack trace was captured. To
+  reproduce, rerun with `--blocks 400 --no-tokens` and sample the hung
+  process.
+- **No real DB dump tool yet.** The dump path was exercised only with a stub
+  tool that lists file names.
+- **Python-built blocks on the V2 tier.** R2 (a competing chain built in
+  Python) is not implemented there and falls back to R1.
+- **Token scenarios** are asserted only for parity, not for the expected
+  token results; the `chronik_token_*` functional tests cover those.
diff --git a/contrib/chronik-parity/compare.py b/contrib/chronik-parity/compare.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..2542503135b8d931d53e1c1b4ca6e3db22714c20
--- /dev/null
+++ b/contrib/chronik-parity/compare.py
@@ -0,0 +1,562 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env python3
+# Copyright (c) 2026 The Lotus developers
+# Distributed under the MIT software license, see the accompanying
+# file COPYING or http://www.opensource.org/licenses/mit-license.php.
+"""Compare Chronik responses of two implementations (A and B).
+
+Used as a library by parity_chain.py / corpus.py, and as a CLI to compare two
+saved response files (JSON lines written by `corpus.py --save-responses`):
+
+    compare.py A.jsonl B.jsonl [--report report.json] [--allow PATH ...]
+
+Each HTTP response is classified as:
+  identical     status, content-type, compared headers and raw body bytes equal
+  parsed_equal  status/content-type/headers equal, bytes differ, but the parsed
+                protobuf messages differ only in allow-listed field paths
+  different     anything else (unexpected difference)
+
+WebSocket sessions are compared message by message (raw bytes first, then the
+parsed `WsMsg`).
+
+Exit status is nonzero if any unexpected difference was found.
+"""
+
+import argparse
+import base64
+import fnmatch
+import json
+import os
+import re
+import subprocess
+import sys
+import tempfile
+from collections import OrderedDict
+from typing import Any, Dict, List, Optional
+
+# Field paths whose difference is accepted. Paths are `Message.field[idx].sub`;
+# patterns are fnmatch'ed against the indexed path and the index-free path.
+DEFAULT_ALLOW = [
+    # FormatFullVersion() of the host build; the same function in both
+    # implementations but may differ between two build trees.
+    "ChronikInfo.version",
+]
+
+# Response headers never compared.
+IGNORED_HEADERS = {"date"}
+
+_pb = None
+
+
+def load_pb(pb2_dir: Optional[str] = None, srcdir: Optional[str] = None):
+    """Import chronik_pb2: from pb2_dir, from sys.path, or generate it with
+    protoc from <srcdir>/chronik/chronik-proto/proto/chronik.proto."""
+    global _pb
+    if _pb is not None:
+        return _pb
+    if pb2_dir:
+        sys.path.insert(0, pb2_dir)
+    try:
+        import chronik_pb2 as pb  # noqa: F401
+    except ImportError:
+        if not srcdir:
+            raise
+        proto_dir = os.path.join(srcdir, "chronik", "chronik-proto", "proto")
+        out = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="chronik-parity-pb2-")
+        subprocess.check_call(
+            ["protoc", f"-I{proto_dir}", f"--python_out={out}", "chronik.proto"]
+        )
+        sys.path.insert(0, out)
+        import chronik_pb2 as pb  # noqa: F401
+    _pb = pb
+    return pb
+
+
+def pb_class(name: Optional[str]):
+    if not name:
+        return None
+    return getattr(load_pb(), name, None)
+
+
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Protobuf diff
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+
+def _strip_idx(path: str) -> str:
+    return re.sub(r"\[\d+\]", "", path)
+
+
+def is_allowed(path: str, allow: List[str]) -> bool:
+    stripped = _strip_idx(path)
+    for pattern in allow:
+        if fnmatch.fnmatchcase(path, pattern) or fnmatch.fnmatchcase(
+            stripped, pattern
+        ):
+            return True
+    return False
+
+
+def _short(value: Any) -> Any:
+    if isinstance(value, bytes):
+        return value.hex() if len(value) <= 64 else value[:64].hex() + "..."
+    if isinstance(value, str) and len(value) > 200:
+        return value[:200] + "..."
+    return value
+
+
+def diff_messages(a, b, path: str, out: List[Dict[str, Any]]) -> None:
+    """Append every differing leaf of two messages of the same type to out."""
+    from google.protobuf.descriptor import FieldDescriptor
+
+    for field in a.DESCRIPTOR.fields:
+        fpath = f"{path}.{field.name}"
+        va = getattr(a, field.name)
+        vb = getattr(b, field.name)
+        is_msg = field.type == FieldDescriptor.TYPE_MESSAGE
+        is_repeated = getattr(field, "is_repeated", None)
+        if is_repeated is None:
+            is_repeated = field.label == FieldDescriptor.LABEL_REPEATED
+        if is_repeated:
+            is_map = is_msg and field.message_type.GetOptions().map_entry
+            if is_map:
+                keys = sorted(set(va.keys()) | set(vb.keys()), key=repr)
+                for key in keys:
+                    kpath = f"{fpath}[{key!r}]"
+                    if key not in va or key not in vb:
+                        out.append({"path": kpath, "a": key in va, "b": key in vb})
+                    elif hasattr(va[key], "DESCRIPTOR"):
+                        diff_messages(va[key], vb[key], kpath, out)
+                    elif va[key] != vb[key]:
+                        out.append(
+                            {"path": kpath, "a": _short(va[key]), "b": _short(vb[key])}
+                        )
+                continue
+            if len(va) != len(vb):
+                out.append({"path": f"{fpath}.<len>", "a": len(va), "b": len(vb)})
+            for i, (ea, eb) in enumerate(zip(va, vb)):
+                ipath = f"{fpath}[{i}]"
+                if is_msg:
+                    diff_messages(ea, eb, ipath, out)
+                elif ea != eb:
+                    out.append({"path": ipath, "a": _short(ea), "b": _short(eb)})
+            continue
+        if is_msg:
+            ha = a.HasField(field.name)
+            hb = b.HasField(field.name)
+            if ha != hb:
+                out.append({"path": f"{fpath}.<present>", "a": ha, "b": hb})
+                continue
+            if ha:
+                diff_messages(va, vb, fpath, out)
+            continue
+        if field.containing_oneof is not None or field.has_presence:
+            try:
+                ha, hb = a.HasField(field.name), b.HasField(field.name)
+                if ha != hb:
+                    out.append({"path": f"{fpath}.<present>", "a": ha, "b": hb})
+                    continue
+            except ValueError:
+                pass
+        if va != vb:
+            out.append({"path": fpath, "a": _short(va), "b": _short(vb)})
+    # Unknown fields (e.g. a field one build knows and the other doesn't)
+    try:
+        ua = a.UnknownFields() if hasattr(a, "UnknownFields") else None
+        ub = b.UnknownFields() if hasattr(b, "UnknownFields") else None
+        if ua is not None and ub is not None:
+            la = [(f.field_number, f.wire_type, f.data) for f in ua]
+            lb = [(f.field_number, f.wire_type, f.data) for f in ub]
+            if la != lb:
+                out.append({"path": f"{path}.<unknown>", "a": len(la), "b": len(lb)})
+    except Exception:
+        pass
+
+
+def parse_body(type_name: Optional[str], body: bytes):
+    cls = pb_class(type_name)
+    if cls is None:
+        return None, "no message type"
+    msg = cls()
+    try:
+        msg.ParseFromString(body)
+    except Exception as err:  # DecodeError
+        return None, f"parse error: {err}"
+    return msg, None
+
+
+PROTOBUF_CT = "application/x-protobuf"
+
+
+def response_type(req: Dict[str, Any], resp: Dict[str, Any]) -> Optional[str]:
+    """Protobuf message type of a response body."""
+    if resp.get("status") is None:
+        return None
+    ctype = (resp.get("headers") or {}).get("content-type", "")
+    if not ctype.startswith(PROTOBUF_CT):
+        return None
+    if resp["status"] == 200:
+        return req.get("pb_type")
+    return "Error"
+
+
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# HTTP comparison
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+
+def _body(resp: Dict[str, Any]) -> bytes:
+    if "body_bytes" in resp:
+        return resp["body_bytes"]
+    if resp.get("body_b64") is not None:
+        return base64.b64decode(resp["body_b64"])
+    if resp.get("body_hex") is not None:
+        return bytes.fromhex(resp["body_hex"])
+    return b""
+
+
+def _cmp_headers(ra, rb):
+    ha = {k: v for k, v in (ra.get("headers") or {}).items() if k not in IGNORED_HEADERS}
+    hb = {k: v for k, v in (rb.get("headers") or {}).items() if k not in IGNORED_HEADERS}
+    if ha == hb:
+        return []
+    out = []
+    for k in sorted(set(ha) | set(hb)):
+        if ha.get(k) != hb.get(k):
+            out.append({"path": f"header:{k}", "a": ha.get(k), "b": hb.get(k)})
+    return out
+
+
+def compare_http(req, ra, rb, allow: List[str]) -> Dict[str, Any]:
+    """Compare one HTTP response pair; returns a result dict with `verdict`."""
+    result: Dict[str, Any] = {
+        "route": req["route"],
+        "method": req["method"],
+        "path": req["path"],
+        "status_a": ra.get("status"),
+        "status_b": rb.get("status"),
+    }
+    diffs: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
+    if ra.get("status") != rb.get("status"):
+        diffs.append({"path": "status", "a": ra.get("status"), "b": rb.get("status")})
+    if ra.get("error") != rb.get("error"):
+        diffs.append({"path": "transport", "a": ra.get("error"), "b": rb.get("error")})
+    header_diffs = _cmp_headers(ra, rb)
+    # content-length follows from the body; the body comparison covers it.
+    header_diffs = [d for d in header_diffs if d["path"] != "header:content-length"]
+    diffs += header_diffs
+    ba, bb = _body(ra), _body(rb)
+    bytes_equal = ba == bb
+    result["bytes_equal"] = bytes_equal
+    if not bytes_equal:
+        ta, tb = response_type(req, ra), response_type(req, rb)
+        if ta and ta == tb:
+            ma, ea = parse_body(ta, ba)
+            mb, eb = parse_body(tb, bb)
+            if ma is not None and mb is not None:
+                pdiffs: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
+                diff_messages(ma, mb, ta, pdiffs)
+                if not pdiffs:
+                    # Same message, different encoding (e.g. field order).
+                    pdiffs.append({"path": f"{ta}.<encoding>", "a": len(ba), "b": len(bb)})
+                diffs += pdiffs
+            else:
+                diffs.append({"path": "body.<parse>", "a": ea, "b": eb})
+        else:
+            diffs.append(
+                {
+                    "path": "body.<raw>",
+                    "a": _short(ba),
+                    "b": _short(bb),
+                }
+            )
+    unexpected = [d for d in diffs if not is_allowed(d["path"], allow)]
+    accepted = [d for d in diffs if is_allowed(d["path"], allow)]
+    if not diffs:
+        result["verdict"] = "identical"
+    elif not unexpected:
+        result["verdict"] = "parsed_equal"
+    else:
+        result["verdict"] = "different"
+    if diffs:
+        result["unexpected"] = unexpected[:50]
+        result["accepted"] = accepted[:50]
+        result["num_diffs"] = len(diffs)
+    return result
+
+
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# WebSocket comparison
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+
+def _ws_frame(frame) -> Dict[str, Any]:
+    if isinstance(frame, dict):
+        return frame
+    opcode, data = frame
+    return {"opcode": opcode, "data": data}
+
+
+def compare_ws(name: str, msgs_a: List, msgs_b: List, allow: List[str],
+               mode: str = "strict"):
+    """Compare two WebSocket message sequences of one session.
+
+    mode "strict": identical sequences. mode "set": identical sets of distinct
+    messages (for sessions with several subscriptions, whose interleaving and
+    consecutive-duplicate suppression are scheduling dependent).
+    """
+    fa = [_ws_frame(m) for m in msgs_a]
+    fb = [_ws_frame(m) for m in msgs_b]
+    result: Dict[str, Any] = {
+        "session": name,
+        "mode": mode,
+        "num_a": len(fa),
+        "num_b": len(fb),
+    }
+    if mode == "set":
+        def key(m):
+            return (m["opcode"], m["data"])
+        uniq_a = sorted({key(m) for m in fa})
+        uniq_b = sorted({key(m) for m in fb})
+        fa = [{"opcode": o, "data": d} for o, d in uniq_a]
+        fb = [{"opcode": o, "data": d} for o, d in uniq_b]
+        result["distinct_a"] = len(fa)
+        result["distinct_b"] = len(fb)
+        if len(fa) != len(fb) or fa != fb:
+            only_a = [d.hex() for o, d in sorted(set(uniq_a) - set(uniq_b))][:20]
+            only_b = [d.hex() for o, d in sorted(set(uniq_b) - set(uniq_a))][:20]
+            result["verdict"] = "different"
+            result["unexpected"] = [{"path": "WsSession.<set>", "a": only_a, "b": only_b}]
+            result["num_identical"] = len(set(uniq_a) & set(uniq_b))
+            return result
+    diffs: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
+    if len(fa) != len(fb):
+        diffs.append({"path": "WsSession.<len>", "a": len(fa), "b": len(fb)})
+    num_identical = 0
+    for i, (ma, mb) in enumerate(zip(fa, fb)):
+        if ma["opcode"] == mb["opcode"] and ma["data"] == mb["data"]:
+            num_identical += 1
+            continue
+        if ma["opcode"] != mb["opcode"]:
+            diffs.append({"path": f"WsSession[{i}].opcode", "a": ma["opcode"], "b": mb["opcode"]})
+            continue
+        pa, ea = parse_body("WsMsg", ma["data"])
+        pb_, eb = parse_body("WsMsg", mb["data"])
+        if pa is None or pb_ is None:
+            diffs.append({"path": f"WsSession[{i}].<parse>", "a": ea, "b": eb})
+            continue
+        pdiffs: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
+        diff_messages(pa, pb_, f"WsMsg[{i}]", pdiffs)
+        if not pdiffs:
+            pdiffs.append({"path": f"WsMsg[{i}].<encoding>", "a": 0, "b": 0})
+        diffs += pdiffs
+    unexpected = [d for d in diffs if not is_allowed(d["path"], allow)]
+    result["num_identical"] = num_identical
+    result["verdict"] = (
+        "identical" if not diffs else ("parsed_equal" if not unexpected else "different")
+    )
+    if diffs:
+        result["unexpected"] = unexpected[:50]
+        result["num_diffs"] = len(diffs)
+    return result
+
+
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Report aggregation
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+
+class Report:
+    """Accumulates comparison results across checkpoints."""
+
+    def __init__(self, allow: List[str]):
+        self.allow = allow
+        self.routes: Dict[str, Dict[str, int]] = OrderedDict()
+        self.ws: Dict[str, int] = {"sessions": 0, "messages": 0, "identical": 0,
+                                   "parsed_equal": 0, "different": 0}
+        self.dumps: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
+        self.failures: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
+        self.accepted: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
+        self.checkpoints: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
+        self.notes: List[str] = []
+
+    def add_http(self, result: Dict[str, Any], checkpoint: str = "") -> None:
+        key = f"{result['method']} {result['route']}"
+        counts = self.routes.setdefault(
+            key, {"requests": 0, "identical": 0, "parsed_equal": 0, "different": 0}
+        )
+        counts["requests"] += 1
+        counts[result["verdict"]] += 1
+        if result["verdict"] == "different":
+            if len(self.failures) < 500:
+                self.failures.append(dict(result, checkpoint=checkpoint))
+        elif result["verdict"] == "parsed_equal":
+            if len(self.accepted) < 200:
+                self.accepted.append(dict(result, checkpoint=checkpoint))
+
+    def add_ws(self, result: Dict[str, Any], checkpoint: str = "") -> None:
+        self.ws["sessions"] += 1
+        self.ws["messages"] += max(result["num_a"], result["num_b"])
+        self.ws[result["verdict"]] += 1
+        if result["verdict"] == "different" and len(self.failures) < 500:
+            self.failures.append(dict(result, checkpoint=checkpoint, kind="ws"))
+
+    def add_dump(self, result: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:
+        self.dumps.append(result)
+        if not result.get("equal", False):
+            self.failures.append(dict(result, kind="dump"))
+
+    def totals(self) -> Dict[str, int]:
+        tot = {"requests": 0, "identical": 0, "parsed_equal": 0, "different": 0}
+        for counts in self.routes.values():
+            for k in tot:
+                tot[k] += counts[k]
+        return tot
+
+    def ok(self) -> bool:
+        return not self.failures
+
+    def to_json(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:
+        return {
+            "ok": self.ok(),
+            "allow": self.allow,
+            "http_totals": self.totals(),
+            "http_routes": self.routes,
+            "ws": self.ws,
+            "dumps": self.dumps,
+            "checkpoints": self.checkpoints,
+            "failures": self.failures,
+            "accepted_differences": self.accepted,
+            "notes": self.notes,
+        }
+
+    def write(self, path: str) -> None:
+        with open(path, "w", encoding="utf8") as f:
+            json.dump(self.to_json(), f, indent=1, default=str)
+
+    def summary(self) -> str:
+        lines = []
+        width = max([len(k) for k in self.routes] + [10])
+        lines.append(
+            f"{'route':<{width}} {'reqs':>7} {'bytes=':>7} {'parsed=':>7} {'diff':>6}"
+        )
+        for key, c in self.routes.items():
+            lines.append(
+                f"{key:<{width}} {c['requests']:>7} {c['identical']:>7} "
+                f"{c['parsed_equal']:>7} {c['different']:>6}"
+            )
+        t = self.totals()
+        lines.append(
+            f"{'TOTAL':<{width}} {t['requests']:>7} {t['identical']:>7} "
+            f"{t['parsed_equal']:>7} {t['different']:>6}"
+        )
+        w = self.ws
+        lines.append(
+            f"WebSocket: {w['sessions']} sessions, {w['messages']} messages; "
+            f"identical {w['identical']}, parsed-equal {w['parsed_equal']}, "
+            f"different {w['different']}"
+        )
+        if self.dumps:
+            neq = sum(1 for d in self.dumps if not d.get("equal"))
+            lines.append(f"DB dumps: {len(self.dumps)} compared, {neq} different")
+        else:
+            lines.append("DB dumps: not run (no --dump-tool)")
+        lines.append(f"Unexpected differences: {len(self.failures)}")
+        return "\n".join(lines)
+
+
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# DB dump comparison
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+
+# meta\t<hex("NATIVE_LAST_WRITER")>
+DUMP_IGNORED_PREFIXES = ("meta\t" + b"NATIVE_LAST_WRITER".hex() + "\t",)
+
+
+def compare_dump_files(path_a: str, path_b: str, label: str, max_lines: int = 50):
+    """Compare two `table\\tkey_hex\\tvalue_hex` dumps; returns a result dict."""
+    import difflib
+
+    with open(path_a, encoding="utf8") as f:
+        la = f.read().splitlines()
+    with open(path_b, encoding="utf8") as f:
+        lb = f.read().splitlines()
+    # The native indexer records which implementation last wrote the DB
+    # (meta NATIVE_LAST_WRITER); Rust never writes it. It is expected to
+    # differ, so it is reported but not compared.
+    ignored = [line for line in la + lb if line.startswith(DUMP_IGNORED_PREFIXES)]
+    la = [line for line in la if not line.startswith(DUMP_IGNORED_PREFIXES)]
+    lb = [line for line in lb if not line.startswith(DUMP_IGNORED_PREFIXES)]
+    result: Dict[str, Any] = {
+        "checkpoint": label,
+        "lines_a": len(la),
+        "lines_b": len(lb),
+        "equal": la == lb,
+        "ignored": ignored,
+    }
+    per_table_a: Dict[str, int] = {}
+    for line in la:
+        t = line.split("\t", 1)[0]
+        per_table_a[t] = per_table_a.get(t, 0) + 1
+    result["tables"] = per_table_a
+    if la != lb:
+        diff = list(
+            difflib.unified_diff(la, lb, "A", "B", n=0, lineterm="")
+        )
+        result["diff_head"] = diff[: max_lines]
+        result["num_diff_lines"] = len(diff)
+    return result
+
+
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# CLI
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+
+def _load_jsonl(path: str) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:
+    with open(path, encoding="utf8") as f:
+        return [json.loads(line) for line in f if line.strip()]
+
+
+def main() -> int:
+    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__,
+                                     formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
+    parser.add_argument("responses_a", help="JSON-lines responses of implementation A")
+    parser.add_argument("responses_b", help="JSON-lines responses of implementation B")
+    parser.add_argument("--report", help="write the JSON report here")
+    parser.add_argument("--allow", action="append", default=[],
+                        help="additional allowed field path (fnmatch pattern)")
+    parser.add_argument("--pb2-dir", help="directory containing chronik_pb2.py")
+    parser.add_argument("--srcdir", help="source tree to generate chronik_pb2 from")
+    args = parser.parse_args()
+    load_pb(args.pb2_dir, args.srcdir)
+    allow = DEFAULT_ALLOW + args.allow
+    report = Report(allow)
+    ra = _load_jsonl(args.responses_a)
+    rb = {(r.get("kind", "http"), r["id"]): r for r in _load_jsonl(args.responses_b)}
+    for entry in ra:
+        kind = entry.get("kind", "http")
+        other = rb.pop((kind, entry["id"]), None)
+        if other is None:
+            report.failures.append({"kind": "missing_in_b", "id": entry["id"]})
+            continue
+        if kind == "ws":
+            msgs_a = [(m["opcode"], base64.b64decode(m["data"])) for m in entry["messages"]]
+            msgs_b = [(m["opcode"], base64.b64decode(m["data"])) for m in other["messages"]]
+            report.add_ws(compare_ws(entry["id"], msgs_a, msgs_b, allow,
+                                     mode=entry.get("mode", "strict")),
+                          entry.get("checkpoint", ""))
+        else:
+            report.add_http(compare_http(entry["request"], entry["response"],
+                                         other["response"], allow),
+                            entry.get("checkpoint", ""))
+    for key in rb:
+        report.failures.append({"kind": "missing_in_a", "id": key[1]})
+    print(report.summary())
+    if args.report:
+        report.write(args.report)
+    return 0 if report.ok() else 1
+
+
+if __name__ == "__main__":
+    sys.exit(main())
